WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.260
A traditional painting is
typically oil on canvas,

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:06.350
protected with a layer
of natural resin varnish.

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:09.410
Over time, a linen
canvas can become

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:12.200
brittle, distort and
sometimes tear.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:15.170
The conservators begin
with spot tests to

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:17.240
observe how different
colors in the painting

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:18.650
react to cleaning.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:22.010
They dip a cotton swab
in mild detergent and rub

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:25.130
gently testing for
discolored varnish and

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:28.370
any previous
restoration. Next, they

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:31.160
spray the back of the
canvas with water and lie

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.200
the painting on a vacuum
hot table.

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:36.470
The heat turns the
moisture to steam, which

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:39.710
relaxes the paint and
linen as the vacuum pulls

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:40.790
the bumps and puckers
flat.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.960
If the canvas isn't
structurally sound, they

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.080
apply adhesive a
synthetic backing, then a

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.500
new linen canvas with
thermometers monitoring

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.750
the temperature. The
heat activates the

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.690
adhesive and the vacuum
draws everything taut

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:58.190
against the hot table.

